{"text": "SS\n\nDanna Diaz, Ph.D\n\nEXECUTIVE PROFILE\nEDUCATIONAL LEADERSHIP \u00bb CULTURE SHAPING \u00bb VISION & GOAL SETTING\n\nNationally Recognized Educational Leader and The University of Texas PhD. | 25+ Years Instructional & Administrative\nExperience, Relationship Building, Instructional Leadership, Family & Community Engagement\n\nCultivates and optimizes talent by identifying and leveraging complementary strengths, and influences meaningful and\n\npurposeful changes in challenging educational environments. Appointed to TASA Major Committees to lead and contribute to\ncritical policy discussions at the state level. Currently exploring opportunities to integrate skills, talent, and passion into a\n\nleadership role for a district facing complex challenges.\n\nCommunity Initiatives * Multicultural Education * Cooperative & Collaborative Teaching Models\nProfessional Learning Communities (PLC) * Learning Academies \u00b0 Instructional Leadership\nCollaborative Decision Making \u00b0 Strategic Planning * Pedagogy Transformation * Team Building\n\na\nPROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE\n\nASSISTANT SUPERINTENDENT, El Paso, TX 2013 -present\nProvide leadership serving approximately 20,000 students in an urban environment. Area one is comprised of a\n\ndemographically diverse student population. Collaborate with district personnel to ensure families, students and community\nmembers are an integral part in the educational process.\n\ne All schools in Area One met state standards, with four schools meeting six distinctions and one meeting all seven distinctions.\n*  PK-12 Vertical focus with establishment of tiered systems of support.\n*  Steered school administrators through capacity building in a complex district, and counseled school leadership\non implementing and adapting to state standards and district initiatives.\nDIRECTOR, STUDENT ENGAGEMENT, Fort Worth, TX 2006 - 2013\nProvided leadership serving approximately 82,000 students in an urban environment. The demographics of the student\npopulation are: 60% Hispanic, 26% African American, 13% White , 1% other and 75 % economically disadvantaged.\ne Increased student councils at the elementary schools from 63% to 92%.\ne Over 50,000 visited Go Centers in the schools, faith organizations, community centers and libraries from 2006-2011.\n\u00a2 Fort Worth was selected as one of the Best 100 Communities for Young People from Americas Promise in 2010.\ne Graduation rates increased by ten percent as cited in the Cities in Crisis Report.\ne Provided leadership in the research, implementation and monitoring of the Children\u2019s Defense Fund Freedom Schools\nPrograms \u00ae for the 2012 & 2013 summer programming. Student's lexile levels increased from 50 \u2014 300 points during the six\nweek program.\nREGIONAL SUPERINTENDENT, Philadelphia, PA 2005 \u2014 2006\nSenior Superintendent of the South Region; strategic accountability and direct daily leadership of a large urban\nschool district with 30 schools and approximately 20,000 students.\n\u00a9 Provided fiscal and administrative oversight to an annual budget and a professional staff of over 1,000 union and non-\nunion employees.\n* Collaborated, developed and executed a campus plan designed around the customized needs of both the school and students\nserved, accelerated student achievement to standards and provided ongoing support to principals, teachers and support staff.\n* Supervised principals of assigned schools and worked closely and collaboratively with them and their leadership team to\nincrease and accelerate the overall effectiveness and efficiency of the instructional service delivery system to significantly\nincrease student achievement.",
